        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Disconnect every hose.</strong> That one step prevents most burst hose-bib pipes, because a connected hose stops a frost-free faucet draining and turns a well-designed spigot into a closed pipe full of water.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then, for a standard spigot, close the indoor shut-off, open the outdoor faucet, drain the section between them through the bleeder cap, and leave the faucet open all winter. Add a cover, and insulate any supply run through an unheated space.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If a spigot has no shut-off and is not frost-free, get one fitted. It is a modest job that permanently removes a burst risk you would otherwise manage with foam and hope every single winter.
          </p>
